    "body_en": "<p>In a National Institutes of Health-funded study, scientists have demonstrated that the drug losartan, commonly prescribed for high blood pressure, boosts brown adipose tissue activity in mice, enhancing their metabolism. The institutional report frames the development in practical terms and ties it to the broader mission or observing effort.</p><p>That matters because biology becomes more informative when an observed effect begins to look like a mechanism rather than an isolated pattern. The gap between identifying a correlation in biological data and understanding the causal chain that produces it is routinely underestimated, and the history of biomedical research is populated with associations that collapsed when the mechanism was sought and not found. A result that comes with a proposed mechanism, even a partial one, is more useful than a purely descriptive finding because it generates testable predictions that can narrow the hypothesis space. In a National Institutes of Health (NIH)-funded study, scientists have demonstrated that the drug losartan, commonly prescribed for high blood pressure, boosts brown adipose. Researchers initially discovered the connection between brown fat and the drug class to which losartan belongs, known as angiotensin receptor blockers (ARBs), through an analysis.</p><p>This indicates that repurposing a widely used drug, with a well-known safety profile, may be a clinically viable method of stimulating brown fat, which has been linked to improved. Rodgers, M. D, director of NIH’s National Institute of Diabetes and Digestive and Kidney Disease (NIDDK).</p><p>By assessing these kinds of images and medical records from more than 50,000 patients being treated for cancer, a research team led by Paul Cohen, M. D, Ph. D, at Rockefeller. We doubled our number of patients and considered more than 500 codes, which allowed us to unearth many new insights.” Cohen, joined by Memorial Sloan Kettering Cancer Center.</p><p>They uncovered some new connections as well, including a lower incidence of anxiety and depression in patients with greater brown fat presence. That way you would be able to stimulate brown fat in people with normal blood pressure,” said first author Nicolás Gómez-Banoy, M. D, an instructor of clinical investigation at.</p><p>The broader interest lies in whether the reported effect points toward a real mechanism and not merely a reproducible but unexplained association. Biology has learned from decades of biomarker failures that correlation, even robust correlation, is not a substitute for mechanistic understanding. A pathway that can be traced from molecular interaction to cellular response to organismal phenotype provides a far stronger foundation for intervention than a statistical association discovered in a large dataset, however well the statistics are done.</p><p>NIH supported this research through NIDDK grant DP1DK139983, National Cancer Institute (NCI) grant P30CA008748, National Institute of General Medical Sciences (NIGMS) grant. About the National Institute of Diabetes and Digestive and Kidney Diseases (NIDDK): The NIDDK, a component of the National Institutes of Health (NIH), conducts and supports.</p><p>Because the account originates with NIH News Releases, it functions best as a primary institutional report that is close to the data and operations, not as independent scientific validation. Institutional communications are produced by organizations with legitimate interests in presenting their work in a favorable light, which does not make them unreliable but does make them partial. Details that complicate the narrative, including instrument limitations, unexpected failures and results below projections, tend to be minimized relative to progress messages. Technical documentation and peer-reviewed publications, where they exist, provide the complementary layer that institutional releases cannot substitute.</p><p>The next step is to test whether the effect repeats across different methods, cell types, model organisms and experimental conditions. Reproducibility is the first test, but mechanistic dissection is the second, and a result that passes both has a substantially better chance of translating into something clinically or biotechnologically useful. The path from a laboratory finding to an applied outcome typically takes a decade or more, and most findings do not complete it; the current result sits at the beginning of that process.</p><p class=\"art-source\"><a href=\"https://www.nih.gov/news-events/news-releases/blood-pressure-drug-may-activate-health-promoting-brown-fat\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Source</a></p>",
